# First-Class Restrictive Unit-Scope Filters

## Objective

Replace the separate, singular `unit_scope` subsystem with ordinary SoulSearch
candidate filters. Unit-scope filters must use the same descriptor, selection,
presentation, clearing, counting, and preset mechanisms as race filters.

The resulting candidate pipeline is:

1. Start with every unique active unit.
2. Apply selected unit-scope candidate filters.
3. Apply selected race candidate filters.
4. Build search rows and apply ranking filters.

## Behavioral contract

- Unit scopes are `SoulSearchFilterDescriptor` entries with candidate behavior.
- No unit-scope filters at all means the scope layer passes every upstream unit.
- If there are no positive unit-scope filters, positive inclusion starts from the
  entire upstream set; any negative scope filters still subtract their matches.
- Positive unit-scope filters are ORed with one another.
- Negative unit-scope filters exclude matching units after positive inclusion.
- Formally, a unit passes the scope family when
  `(there are no positive scopes OR it matches any positive scope)` AND
  `it matches no negative scope`.
- Race filters retain the same positive-OR/negative-exclusion semantics.
- Candidate-filter families compose by intersection. For example:
  `(Residents OR Visitors) AND (Humanoids OR Dwarves)`.
- `All units` is represented by the absence of all unit-scope filters. A
  negative-only scope selection instead means `All units except ...`.
- `All units` is not a descriptor that can be selected alongside other scopes.
- Unit-scope filters appear in the Active filters list, contribute to `Filters: N`,
  are removed by Clear filters, and are saved and loaded with filter presets.
- The primary window still receives Humanoids as its first-open default filter. It
  receives no default unit-scope filter, so removing Humanoids exposes all active
  units.
- Scoped Creatures-menu windows seed an ordinary unit-scope filter plus any race
  filter applicable to that tab instead of passing a separate `unit_scope` option.

## Scope boundaries

- Included: multiple simultaneously selected unit-scope filters. This follows
  naturally from making scopes ordinary filters and fulfills the adjacent
  multi-scope project item.
- Included: high/include and low/exclude directions for unit-scope filters, matching
  other candidate filters.
- Included: compatibility normalization for the existing
  `ui.open({unit_scope=...})` caller contract.
- Excluded: adding the planned Wildlife, Livestock, and Pets scope definitions.
- Excluded: changing the membership predicates of the existing Citizens,
  Residents, Citizens and pets, and Visitors scopes; this refactor must preserve
  their currently characterized behavior.
- Excluded: changing the primary window's Humanoids first-open default.
- Excluded: file-backed window state; window settings remain session-only.
- Do not hand-edit `dist/`; regenerate it with the repository build tooling.

## Phase 0 - Characterize and lock the filter algebra

- [x] Run `./tools/Test.ps1` and record the clean baseline below.
- [x] Add green characterization coverage for the current composition path:
  scalar unit scope -> race provider -> resident row collection.
- [x] Record the replacement filter-algebra test matrix for Phases 1 and 2:
  - no scope filters passes every active unit;
  - one positive scope includes only matching units;
  - multiple positive scopes use union semantics;
  - negative-only scope filters subtract from all active units;
  - positive and negative scope filters produce include-except results;
  - scope and race families intersect;
  - duplicate and inactive active-list entries never reach results;
  - upstream errors return no partial candidates.
- [x] Fix stable descriptor IDs and serialized values as:
  `unit_scope:citizens`, `unit_scope:fort_residents`,
  `unit_scope:citizens_and_pets`, and `unit_scope:visitors`, matching the
  serialized `UNIT_SCOPE='unit_scope'` kind.
- [x] Lock legacy conversion precedence:
  - `unit_scope='all_active'` adds no scope filter;
  - another valid scalar adds the matching positive scope filter;
  - explicit `options.filters` containing a unit-scope descriptor are canonical
    and take precedence over a simultaneous legacy scalar;
  - when `options.filters` do not specify a unit scope, an explicit legacy scalar
    replaces unit-scope filters inherited from saved settings, preserving the old
    per-field override behavior;
  - malformed legacy values are ignored without mutating canonical filters;
  - conversion never mutates caller-owned option/filter tables.
- [x] Confirm that old `window_settings` snapshots need no migration: settings are
  module-local/session-only and are cleared when the changed modules reload.
  Compatibility is for explicit caller options, not hidden persistence.

Phase 0 gate:

- [x] The green baseline and current-path characterization are recorded, and the
  implementation test matrix unambiguously defines empty, positive, negative,
  multi-scope, cross-family, and compatibility behavior without intentionally
  failing Phase 0 tests.

## Phase 1 - Add descriptors and family-filter providers

- [ ] Add `FILTER_CONSTANTS.kind.UNIT_SCOPE='unit_scope'` plus matching
  `unit_scope:` descriptor prefix/keys to `filter_constants.lua`.
- [ ] Reuse `FILTER_CONSTANTS.unit_scope` as the canonical descriptor namespace for
  its prefix and concrete keys. Mark only the scalar-only `ALL_ACTIVE` sentinel and
  `default_unit_scope` as legacy, keeping them functional until Phase 2 removes the
  old provider and its callers.
- [ ] Create `unit_scope_catalog.lua`, parallel to `race_catalog.lua`, to own:
  - stable descriptors and player-facing labels;
  - Citizens, Residents, Citizens and pets, and Visitors matching predicates;
  - descriptor lookup/matching without world-derived cache.
- [ ] Add unit-scope descriptors to `descriptors.lua` groups, flat ordering, and
  `by_id` lookup with `behavior=CANDIDATE`.
- [ ] Add `active_unit_provider.lua` to own active-unit enumeration, availability
  checks, source ordering, and duplicate suppression. Keep the existing
  `candidate_provider.lua` as the generic provider wrapper.
- [ ] Add `candidate_filter_family_provider.lua` for the shared
  positive-OR/negative-exclusion algorithm. Its inputs are an upstream provider,
  selected filters, a descriptor kind, and a unit matcher.
- [ ] Make the shared family provider ignore invalid, stale, and other-kind filter
  entries while preserving source order, duplicate suppression, and upstream error
  propagation.
- [ ] Refactor `race_filter_provider.lua` into a thin adapter over that shared helper
  without changing its behavior.
- [ ] Add `unit_scope_filter_provider.lua` as the equivalent thin adapter for
  `UNIT_SCOPE` descriptors.
- [ ] Keep the inclusive scalar `unit_scope_provider.lua` temporarily as the
  characterized legacy path until Phase 2 migrates its production callers.
- [ ] Keep unit-scope and race matching in separate providers so sequential
  composition gives explicit AND semantics between filter families.
- [ ] Confirm at the domain-state layer that candidate descriptors cannot be moved
  as ranking filters; presentation priority is handled in Phase 3.
- [ ] Update module registration/lifecycle metadata for added, renamed, or removed
  modules.
- [ ] Add `unit_scope_catalog_test.lua`, `active_unit_provider_test.lua`,
  `candidate_filter_family_provider_test.lua`, and
  `unit_scope_filter_provider_test.lua`; register every new suite in
  `tests/run.lua`.
- [ ] Update `filter_constants_test.lua`, descriptor/filter-state tests,
  `race_filter_provider_test.lua`, `candidate_provider_test.lua`,
  `module_registry_test.lua`, metadata tests, and lifecycle tests as applicable.

Phase 1 gate:

- [ ] Focused catalog/provider tests pass for empty, positive, negative, union, and
  include-except behavior in each family. Cross-family intersection remains a
  Phase 2 composition test.

## Phase 2 - Atomically cut over state, callers, and UI

This phase is intentionally one vertical cutover. Removing session scope APIs while
the dedicated scope UI still calls them would leave an unloadable intermediate
checkout, so the domain and UI switch must land together.

- [ ] Remove `unit_scope` storage, getters, setters, and validation from
  `search_session.lua`; all scope selections must live in its existing
  `filter_state`.
- [ ] Remove `unit_scope` snapshots from `window_settings.lua` and canonical resolved
  settings from `window_config.lua`.
- [ ] Teach `window_config.lua` to translate legacy scalar `unit_scope` options into
  ordinary selected filters according to the Phase 0 precedence rules before final
  `filter_state` validation.
- [ ] Preserve caller-owned inputs and filter ordering while preventing duplicate
  scope IDs during legacy translation.
- [ ] Keep the primary default exactly one positive Humanoids filter and no default
  unit-scope filter.
- [ ] Change `creatures_menu_scope.lua` presets to emit a single `filters` list
  containing their scope descriptor and any race descriptor configured for that
  tab; Visitors currently has no race descriptor.
- [ ] Update `main_window.lua:refresh_candidates()` to compose:
  active-unit provider -> unit-scope filter provider -> race filter provider.
- [ ] Pass the same isolated snapshot from
  `SearchSession:get_candidate_filters()` to both family providers; each adapter
  resolves only descriptors of its own kind.
- [ ] Verify filter presets require no schema change: their existing serialized
  `filters` array should automatically preserve unit-scope descriptor IDs and
  directions.
- [ ] Document and test that loading any custom, built-in, or role preset continues
  to replace the complete filter list. A preset without unit-scope descriptors
  therefore restores unrestricted unit scope instead of retaining hidden state.
- [ ] Remove the `Search for: <scope>` label, Edit button, and singular
  `UnitScopePicker` path from the filter panel in the same cutover.
- [ ] Add an `Add unit scope filter` control backed by the existing
  `SearchablePicker` pattern.
- [ ] Add `UNIT_SCOPE` to `SEARCH_QUERY_KIND`, a `unit_scope_query` session field,
  and the corresponding main-window/filter-panel query callbacks and refresh path.
- [ ] Add the unit-scope picker to `PICKER_VIEW_IDS` and every picker open/close/
  visibility loop; remove the obsolete singular scope picker entry.
- [ ] Present unit-scope descriptors through the existing filter toggle/direction
  path so selections immediately live in shared `filter_state`.
- [ ] Generalize active-filter presentation and mouse actions from race-kind checks
  to `behavior == CANDIDATE`, including suppression of ranking move controls and
  their hit targets.
- [ ] Resolve ranking priority from catalog descriptor behavior rather than
  nonexistent `kind` fields on `SoulSearchSelectedFilter` values.
- [ ] Add unit-scope category color, include/exclude tooltips, empty-state guidance,
  and picker help without synthesizing an `All units` filter row.
- [ ] Update `tests/support/soulsearch_env.lua` stubs to expose the new descriptor,
  provider, and settings contracts.
- [ ] Remove `unit_scope_provider.lua` and its old test/harness loader only after all
  production callers use the new pipeline and its behavior has replacement
  coverage in the active-unit/catalog/filter-provider suites.
- [ ] Remove the scalar-only `ALL_ACTIVE` sentinel and `default_unit_scope` after the
  old provider is gone. Retain the `unit_scope` descriptor namespace, concrete keys,
  and prefix; keep any `all_active` compatibility string private to the
  window-config adapter.
- [ ] Remove obsolete scope presenter/formatter/layout helpers and the dedicated
  picker module, and update module registry metadata in the same cutover.
- [ ] Update `filter_state_test.lua`, `filter_presets_test.lua`,
  `search_session_test.lua`, `window_config_test.lua`,
  `window_settings_test.lua`, `creatures_menu_scope_test.lua`, and
  `ui_open_test.lua`, plus the focused filter-panel/presenter/action tests required
  to keep the main window loadable.
- [ ] Add composition coverage proving unit-scope and race families intersect while
  ranking filters receive only the already-filtered rows.

Phase 2 gate:

- [ ] There is no canonical `unit_scope` field outside the explicit legacy adapter.
- [ ] Default, reopened, same-identity, preset-loaded, and Creatures-scoped windows
  derive unit restrictions exclusively from their ordinary filter lists.
- [ ] Legacy scalar open options resolve according to the documented precedence
  without becoming stored canonical state.
- [ ] The main window loads with the new picker and no old scope control/API/module
  dependency remains.

## Phase 3 - Harden unified filter UI and regression coverage

- [ ] Add picker characterization for open/close exclusivity, query updates,
  selected markers, repeated toggles, and multiple simultaneous scope filters.
- [ ] Add mixed scope/race/ranking active-list tests proving candidate filters do not
  consume ranking priority numbers or expose ranking movement controls.
- [ ] Test candidate-filter mouse hit zones so hidden move actions cannot dispatch,
  while include, exclude, and remove remain independently clickable.
- [ ] Test unit-scope row colors and tooltips without falling through to attribute
  descriptions or race-only copy.
- [ ] Test that Clear filters removes unit-scope filters, returns the candidate set
  to all active units, and updates `Filters: N` without special accounting.
- [ ] Test that loading a preset with no scope descriptors removes active scope
  filters and refreshes picker markers, candidates, results, and stats once.
- [ ] Keep picker frames and filter-panel layout usable after replacing the old
  two-control scope row.
- [ ] Update `filter_action_list_test.lua`, `filter_panel_test.lua`,
  filter-presenter coverage, `search_session_test.lua`, `ui_format_test.lua`,
  `ui_layout_test.lua`, `ui_characterization_test.lua`, and
  `widget_extensions_test.lua` as applicable.
- [ ] Audit `module_registry.lua`, `tests/run.lua`, and the test harness for stale old
  scope-provider/picker entries or missing new modules/suites.
- [ ] Run `./tools/Test.ps1` and record the Phase 3 regression result below.

Phase 3 gate:

- [ ] The full automated suite is green, and unit scopes are indistinguishable from
  other candidate filters in selection, active-list actions, count, clearing,
  preset ownership, and multi-selection.

## Phase 4 - Integration, documentation, and package validation

- [ ] Add end-to-end candidate tests for:
  - no scope/no race filters -> all active units;
  - Humanoids only -> all active humanoids;
  - Residents plus Humanoids -> resident humanoids;
  - Residents OR Visitors plus Humanoids -> humanoids in either scope;
  - Residents except Citizens;
  - negative-only Visitors -> all active non-visitors.
- [ ] Prove filter preset save/load round-trips mixed scope, race, and ranking filters
  without format changes or stale references.
- [ ] Prove loading a preset with no unit-scope descriptor clears previously active
  unit-scope filters because preset application replaces the unified filter list.
- [ ] Prove each Creatures-menu integration emits and opens with the intended unified
  filter list.
- [ ] Update README documentation to describe unit scopes as candidate filters,
  empty scope-filter state as All units, negative-only `All except` behavior, and
  cross-family intersection.
- [ ] Update Lua annotations/comments that describe a singular unit scope or an
  inclusive provider.
- [ ] Search source and tests for scalar scope-state signatures such as
  `.unit_scope`, `unit_scope=`, `get_unit_scope`, `set_unit_scope`,
  `default_unit_scope`, and `ALL_ACTIVE`. Only the documented legacy option adapter
  and its compatibility tests may retain scalar option references; catalog/module/
  descriptor names and `unit_scope:` filter IDs are expected to remain.
- [ ] Run `./tools/Test.ps1` and record the result below.
- [ ] Run `./tools/Build.ps1` and record the result below.
- [ ] Run `./tools/VerifyPackage.ps1` and record the result below.
- [ ] Perform live DFHack smoke validation:
  - the primary window starts with Humanoids and no unit-scope filter;
  - removing Humanoids exposes all active units;
  - multiple positive unit scopes broaden within the scope family;
  - a negative unit scope subtracts matching units;
  - adding a race filter intersects with the selected unit scopes;
  - Clear filters returns to all active units;
  - filter counts and saved presets include unit-scope filters;
  - loading a preset without scope filters returns to unrestricted unit scope;
  - each Creatures-menu scoped button seeds the correct filters.
- [ ] Mark both the restrictive-scope and multiple-scope items in
  `docs/project.todo` complete only when all required local validation passes;
  record any deferred live-only checks explicitly.

Phase 4 gate:

- [ ] Source, automated tests, generated package, documentation, and live behavior
  treat unit scopes as first-class candidate filters with no parallel scope state.

